# /usr/data/anvil/anvil.conf -- runtime switches. Edit on the printer, reboot.
# PRESERVED across mod updates.

# FIVE SWITCHES USED TO BE IN THIS FILE and are not any more: MOD_WEB (the web
# stack), MOD_CAM (the camera), MOD_UI (the touchscreen), MOD_WIFI (wlan0) and
# MOD_SSH (which was never read on the printer at all). All five defaulted to
# 1, and what each really offered was a partly-installed mod -- a state nobody
# chose on purpose and everything downstream had to keep a second answer for.
# The web stack, the screen and wifi now start because they are installed, and
# not starting them is `opkg remove`, or a stock FlashForge package.
#
# The camera is the one worth spelling out. mjpg-streamer on :8080, proxied by
# nginx at /webcam/, from FlashForge's own binary on the firmware partition. It
# is an s6-rc longrun that reports ready only once it has a device, so a
# printer without one waits out its timeout-up on every boot.
#
# firmwareExe still reads MOD_CAM when it expands the ok-all bundle, defaulting
# to 1. It is not shipped here because none of these switches is: a
# half-installed mod is not a state to offer. Add MOD_CAM=0 by hand on a
# machine that has no camera and never will.
#
# These tune it when there IS one; defaults shown:
#MOD_CAM_RES=1280x720
#MOD_CAM_FPS=30
#MOD_CAM_PORT=8080

# Boot splash: a line of text on the panel from the moment the mod gets
# control until HelixScreen paints. Without it the screen is black for the
# whole of boot, every boot. FlashForge's own /usr/prog/start.img cannot fill
# that gap -- the only two lines that would display it, both in
# app_startup.sh, are commented out on this firmware. 0 = leave it black.
MOD_SPLASH=1

# Startup: bin/ff-startup.py, run by the firmwareExe wrapper just before the
# UI. EVERY boot it hands the toolhead boards over from their bootloaders,
# starts klipper, and waits for klipper + moonraker to be ready -- naming on
# the panel whichever board or service is holding things up, and handing the
# boards over again on each retry, which is what gives one that missed its
# window another chance. MOD_KLIPPER_TRIES (below) bounds the retries.
#
# 0 = do not wait for the printer: klipper still starts (it is a service in
# its own right and has never depended on this program), the MCU bring-up still
# happens (it is klipper's dependency), and the screen comes up at once in
# front of a machine that may still be connecting. What is skipped is the wait,
# the retries when a board did not answer, the panel narration and the
# first-boot import. That is also what happens automatically if the script or
# the interpreter is missing -- klipper starting must never depend on python.
#MOD_STARTUP_TIMEOUT=240
MOD_STARTUP=1

# First-boot migration, done by the same program once the printer is up: it
# runs FF_IMPORT_FIRMWARE_CONFIG and SAVE_CONFIG to move this unit's factory
# calibration into printer.cfg, then stamps .firmware-config-imported so it
# never runs again. 0 = never migrate (you calibrate by hand); the startup
# wait above still happens.
MOD_IMPORT=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Priorities. Nice values (0..19) for the services that share this SoC's two
# cores with klippy. Higher = yields more.
#
# Why they exist. Stock ran neither moonraker nor nginx nor the streamer --
# both lines are commented out in FlashForge's own start.sh -- so klippy had
# the machine to itself. The mod runs all of them. When the host cannot meet
# Klipper's step-generation deadlines the printer does not slow down, it
# STOPS: the MCU reports "Timer too close" and shuts down mid-job.
#
# Why niceing these is only insurance, not a cure. klippy is already ahead of
# them on both counts: klipper_pri.sh puts its main thread on SCHED_FIFO 50
# (real-time, which outranks every nice value there is), and its serial reader
# thread calls os.nice(-20) itself -- FlashForge's own change, in
# serialhdl.py's _bg_thread. So these numbers only matter for whatever klippy
# thread is neither, and for the case where the chrt or the nice did not take.
# Check that first, it is one line in the log:
#     grep -i nice /usr/data/logs/printer.log
# "current nice = -20" means it applied; "nice process failed" means it did not.
#
# 0 disables (starts the service at normal priority, as before this existed).
# Neither touches memory or eMMC contention -- this printer's busybox has no
# ionice applet, so there is no knob here for either.
#
# Moonraker: modest. It is the print path -- HelixScreen and Mainsail both
# start jobs through it -- so starving it delays the UI, not just background
# work.
NICE_MOONRAKER=5
# The streamer: the most deserving. It is pure background work that nothing
# waits on, and if the camera hands over YUYV rather than MJPEG it encodes
# every frame in software, which is the largest CPU load the mod can add.
# If a print dies with "Timer too close" and this is the difference, the camera
# was the cause: stop it (`S65camera stop`) or unplug it, rather than reaching
# for a nicer number.
NICE_CAM=10
# klippy ITSELF, which is the odd one out: the two above yield TO klippy, this
# one is klippy. 0 -- normal priority -- is what FlashForge shipped and what you
# want; it is here because the number has to live somewhere, and a nice value
# belongs with the other nice values rather than inside the klipperDaemon shim,
# where it used to be and where it made that file machine-specific.
#
# A NEGATIVE value is the only one worth considering and it is mostly redundant:
# klipper_pri.sh already puts klippy's main thread on SCHED_FIFO 50, which
# outranks every nice value there is, and its serial reader thread calls
# os.nice(-20) itself. Set this only if the log says the chrt did not take.
NICE_KLIPPER=0
